 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Performance:** One geometry unit generates 26 to 36 million transformed locations per second [S1].
-*   **Memory Bandwidth:** Achieves up to 640 MBytes/second using commercial DRAMs, Fast Page Mode, alternate memory bank read-out, and a 1-entry cache [S1].
-*   **Memory Constraints:** Maximal original dataset size is $256^3$ for 16-bit voxels with 4Mbit ORAMs, or $512 \times 512 \times 256$ for 16Mbit DRAMs. The maximal resampled dataset size is limited to $512^3$ by the gradient buffer size [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** High throughput for geometry operations; conflict-free memory access; real-time reloadable LUTs and memories [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Fixed hardware functionality; limited by gradient buffer size for resampled data; potential artifacts from discrete weight resolution [S1].
+*   **Performance:** One geometry unit can generate 26 to 36 million transformed locations per second using true 3D resampling [S1].
+*   **Memory Bandwidth:** Achieves up to 640 MB/s access rates using commercial DRAMs, enabled by conflict-free 8-neighbor access, Fast Page Mode, and alternate memory bank read-out [S1].
+*   **Memory Constraints:**
+    *   Original dataset size is limited by available RAM: $256^3$ for 4 Mbit ORAMs, $512 \times 512 \times 256$ for 16 Mbit DRAMs [S1].
+    *   Resampled dataset size is limited by the gradient buffer to $512^3$ [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Offloads the most computationally intensive part of ray casting (geometry and resampling) from the CPU, allowing for real-time visualization of large biomedical datasets [S1, S2]. The use of pre-calculated weights and dedicated hardware ensures high throughput [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Fixed-function hardware lacks the flexibility of programmable shaders. The 6-bit weight resolution, while sufficient for invisibility of artifacts, limits interpolation precision [S1]. The method is tied to the specific VIRIM architecture and is not a general-purpose GPU technique [S1, S2].
 
 ## Background
-This method was developed as part of the Heidelberg Raytracer and VIRIM (Volume Rendering Image Machine) architecture [S1]. It addresses the computational bottleneck of geometry operations, which consume 80% of computation time in software implementations [S1]. The approach uses special-purpose hardware for perspective calculations, gradient estimation, and resampling [S1].
+The method originates from the VIRIM (Volume Rendering Image Machine) project, developed at the University of Mannheim and later commercialized by Volume Graphics GmbH [S1, S2]. It was designed to address the high computational cost of geometry operations, which accounted for 80% of the computation time in software implementations of the basic visualization algorithm [S1]. The architecture was presented in the mid-1990s as a specialized hardware solution for real-time volume rendering [S1].
 
